The factors influencing mushroom growth and yield can be categorized into intrinsic and extrinsic factors. Intrinsic factors pertain to the biological and chemical characteristics of the mushroom and its substrate, including substrate type, carbon to nitrogen ratio, pH level, nitrogen content, moisture content and carbon content. Each of these elements plays a crucial role in determining the nutritional balance and overall health of the mushroom [15]. On the other hand, extrinsic factors refer to environmental conditions that impact mushroom cultivation, such as temperature, humidity, and light. Temperature affects metabolic rates and developmental stages, while humidity influences fruiting and yield. Although mushrooms do not perform photosynthesis, light can still affect specific developmental processes [16]. A comprehensive understanding of both intrinsic and extrinsic factors is essential for optimizing cultivation practices and enhancing mushroom yields. A schematic diagram of the sequential stages of mushroom cultivation is shown in Fig. 1. The process started with the collection and identification of mushroom specimens in key phases such as spawn and substrate production, harvesting of mature mushrooms, and nutritional analysis.

Fig. 1Schematic representation of the workflow for assessing the nutritional value of cultivated mushrooms. The process begins with mushroom cultivation, followed by sample preparation and analysis using laboratory equipment. Nutritional profiling includes the determination of protein content, calcium levels, fiber content, polysaccharides, carbohydrates, and ash content to evaluate the overall nutritional quality of the mushrooms

Shiitake (L. edodes) 868 is a strain known for its early maturation at medium-low temperatures, thriving within the range of 8 to 25 °C. Strain maintained in the edible mushroom laboratory at the Wandong Comprehensive East Anhui Experimental Station of the New Rural Development Research Institute, Anhui Agricultural University, China. Potato dextrose agar (PDA) prepared from raw ingredients has proven more effective than commercially prepared media. To make PDA, 200 g of peeled potatoes were boiled in 500 ml of distilled water, while 20 g of agar was melted separately in another 500 ml of water. The boiled potatoes were strained into the agar solution, supplemented with 20 g of dextrose, mixed, and sterilized by autoclaving. The resulting media was poured into sterile petri plates under laminar airflow. Pure cultures of shiitake strain 868 were inoculated onto these plates and incubated at 24 °C until fully colonized [17]. The cultural slants were stored at 4 °C for future use.

The effectiveness of five different media, yeast malt extract agar (YMEA), corn meal agar (CMA), potato dextrose agar (PDA), malt extract agar (MEA) and potato sucrose agar (PSA), was evaluated to determine the most suitable medium for cultivating L. edodes. The media were prepared following their standard compositions. Subsequently, the petri-plates were inoculated, incubated at 24 °C, and periodic observations were made to monitor the growth [18]. Five liquid media, potato dextrose broth (PD), malt extract broth (ME), oatmeal broth (OM), potato sucrose broth (PS), and yeast extract sucrose broth (YES), were prepared for the purpose of observing the growth of L. eddoes. Inoculations were carried out with 5 ml of mycelial suspension from the test fungus into 100 ml conical flasks containing 50 ml of sterilized media. The flasks were then incubated at 24 °C, and the mycelium’s growth was observed after 20 days of incubation [19]. Both the fresh and dry weight of the mycelium on each medium were recorded. In an oven at 60 °C, the mycelial mat was dried and its dry weight was recorded [20].

This cultivation recipe comprises 89% sawdust, 10% wheat bran, and 1% Lime. To initiate the process, a specific quantity of the cultivation material was accurately measured based on the prescribed formula ratio, thoroughly mixed to ensure a homogeneous blend. Water was added and stirred thoroughly to maintain the material-to-liquid ratio within the range of 1:1.1 to 1:1.2 [21]. The prepared mixture was transferred into a glass bottle and autoclaved at 121 °C for 2 h. The autoclaved mixture was placed in a constant-temperature incubator set at 24 °C until the mycelium was fully developed for subsequent use.

The collected wormwood (A. argyi) straw and stevia (S. rebaudiana) straw residue were dried and crushed into pellets with a pulverizer and stacked for later use, while preparing enough wood chips, wheat bran, lime, and fungus bags. Each raw material was stacked in a ventilated and dry place to ensure that it was not damp, without mold or deterioration. The crushed materials, wormwood (A. argyi) and stevia (S. rebaudiana) residues, were employed to substitute sawdust in 15%, 30%, 45%, and 60% ratios. The conventional pure woodchip formula served as the control. The specific substrate formulation is displayed in Table 1. Crushed A. argyi, S. rebaudiana, sawdust, and excipients were blended per a defined formula. Water (1:1.1 to 1.2 ratio) was added, and the pH was adjusted to 6.5 with lime and potassium dihydrogen phosphate [22]. The mixture was packed into 10 × 25 × 0.04 cm polyethylene bags, sealed, and autoclaved at 121 °C for over 2 h. Under aseptic conditions, bags were inoculated with sawdust spawn in a laminar hood (one bottle per 7–8 bags) and arranged with a neck ring to prevent contamination. Inoculated bags were incubated at 24 °C with 8 + hours of light and elevated CO₂ [23].

