Mushroom growth parameters were measured including: the duration (in days) required for complete mycelial run rate (MRR), mycelial growth in liquid media, mycelium colonization of the substrate in transparent polyethylene bags, stalk length (mm), stalk diameter (mm), pileus diameter (mm), fresh weight of the fruiting bodies (g), the number of harvested fruiting bodies, biological efficiency and total yield (g/bag). Biological efficiency was calculated as the total yield obtained from an individual bag divided by the dry weight of the substrate used, multiplied by 100, and expressed as a percentage.

Experimental data were statistically analyzed as appropriate. Each treatment was replicated five times. Treatment effects were evaluated for significance using either the Completely Randomized Design (CRD) following the procedures outlined by Gomez and Gomez [24].

Protein levels in the mushroom sample were assessed using the Micro-Kjeldahl technique. Reagents included N/100 M HCl, a 4% boric acid solution, 40% sodium hydroxide solution, and a digestion mixture of K2SO4 to CuSO4 in a 5:1 ratio. Samples were digested with sulfuric acid and heated to 420 °C until colorless or faintly green/blue. Distillation followed by sodium hydroxide, capturing ammonia gas in a boric acid solution with a mixed indicator. Ammonium tetraborate formed and was titrated against 0.2 N H2SO4 to a light pink endpoint [25].\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
